S=[
('bethlehem','A','Bethlehem',31.704,35.202,'S',['israel'],["Luke 2:4–7","Matthew 2:1–6"],
 "He is born in Bethlehem of Judaea, as the chief priests and scribes answer Herod from the prophet: a Governor “that shall rule my people Israel.” The flight into Egypt (Matthew 2:13–15) lies off this map, and the return ends at Nazareth (Matthew 2:19–23).",[]),
('nazareth0','A','Nazareth',32.699,35.304,'S',[],["Luke 2:39–40","Luke 2:51–52"],
 "The family returns to Nazareth, where the child grows. At twelve He is “subject unto them”, and He “increased in wisdom and stature.”",[]),
('jerusalem12','A','Jerusalem, at twelve',31.778,35.235,'S',['israel','feast'],["Luke 2:41–50"],
 "At the Passover He stays behind in the temple, among the teachers, and answers His mother with the question “wist ye not that I must be about my Father’s business?” The Gospels record nothing else of His boyhood.",["The Gospels give no other event between this and the Jordan."]),
('jordan1','B','The Jordan',31.837,35.551,'I',['israel'],["Matthew 3:13–17","John 1:28–34"],
 "John baptizes Him in the Jordan, the heavens open, and a voice says, “This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased.” John’s Gospel names the place as Bethabara beyond Jordan.",["The KJV’s “Bethabara” (John 1:28) is a place whose site is not settled; the marker is the map’s placement near Jericho."]),
('wilderness','B','The Wilderness',31.800,35.390,'I',[],["Matthew 4:1–11"],
 "He is led up of the Spirit into the wilderness, fasts forty days and is tempted. The tempter sets Him on a pinnacle of the temple and on an “exceeding high mountain,” and each time He answers from what is written.",["The wilderness and the mountain are not named. The marker is the map’s placement in the wilderness between Jerusalem and Jericho."]),
('jordan2','B','The Jordan again: the first disciples',31.837,35.551,'I',['israel'],["John 1:35–51"],
 "John points to Him and says, “Behold the Lamb of God!” Andrew and another follow; Simon, Philip and Nathanael are called, and Nathanael says, “thou art the King of Israel.” The next day He goes forth into Galilee.",[]),
('cana1','B','Cana of Galilee',32.747,35.341,'S',['sign'],["John 2:1–11"],
 "At a wedding He turns water into wine. John calls it the “beginning of miracles” and writes that He “manifested forth his glory.”",["Cana of Galilee is named; its modern site is disputed, and the marker is the traditional one."]),
('capernaum0','B','Capernaum, a few days',32.881,35.575,'S',[],["John 2:12"],
 "He goes down to Capernaum with His mother, His brethren and His disciples and stays “not many days.”",[]),
('jerusalem1','B','Jerusalem: the first Passover',31.778,35.235,'S',['israel','feast','sign','teach'],["John 2:13–25","John 3:1–21"],
 "At the Passover He drives the sellers out of the temple and answers the Jews, “Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up.” Many believe; Nicodemus, a ruler of the Jews, comes to Him by night.",["John places this cleansing at the first Passover and Matthew 21 places one in the last week. The map reads them as two cleansings; the Gospels do not say whether they are one event told twice."]),
('judaea','B','Judaea: baptizing',31.950,35.250,'I',['israel'],["John 3:22–36"],
 "He and His disciples go into the land of Judaea and baptize, while John is baptizing in Aenon near Salim. John says, “He must increase, but I must decrease.”",["Aenon near Salim is named but not located with certainty; the marker is the map’s placement in the Judaean countryside."]),
('sychar','B','Sychar, in Samaria',32.215,35.288,'S',['gentile','teach'],["John 4:1–42"],
 "He “must needs go through Samaria” and speaks with a Samaritan woman at Jacob’s well. He tells her “salvation is of the Jews”, and many of the city believe and call Him “the Saviour of the world.”",["Matthew 10:5 later forbids the Twelve to enter a city of the Samaritans. The map reports both and does not harmonize them."]),
('cana2','B','Cana again',32.747,35.341,'S',['sign'],["John 4:43–54"],
 "A nobleman of Capernaum comes to Him at Cana; He says, “Go thy way; thy son liveth,” and the boy is healed at a distance. John counts it “the second miracle” He did when He came out of Judaea into Galilee.",[]),
('nazareth1','B','Nazareth: the synagogue',32.699,35.304,'S',['israel','teach'],["Luke 4:16–30"],
 "In the synagogue He reads Isaiah 61 and says, “This day is this scripture fulfilled in your ears.” They marvel, then turn on Him and lead Him to the brow of the hill; He passes through the midst of them. “No prophet is accepted in his own country.”",["Matthew 13:54–58 and Mark 6:1–6 record a visit to His own country with a different outcome. The map reads these as two visits; the Gospels do not say."]),
('jerusalemfeast','C','Jerusalem: a feast of the Jews',31.778,35.235,'S',['israel','feast','sign','teach'],["John 5:1–47"],
 "At “a feast of the Jews” He heals a man at the pool of Bethesda who had been ill thirty-eight years, on the sabbath, and the Jews seek to kill Him. He answers from the Father’s works and from the witness of Moses and the scriptures.",["John does not name the feast, and the place of this visit in the sequence is the map’s own reading."]),
('capernaum1','C','Capernaum: His own city',32.881,35.575,'S',['israel','sign','teach'],["Matthew 4:13–22","Mark 1:21–34","Mark 2:1–12"],
 "He leaves Nazareth and dwells in Capernaum. By the sea He calls Simon, Andrew, James and John to be fishers of men; in the synagogue He casts out an unclean spirit; He heals Peter’s wife’s mother; and He forgives and heals a paralytic let down through the roof.",["The Gospels do not date this order closely; the map follows Matthew and Mark."]),
('galilee1','C','Galilee: the first circuit',32.800,35.450,'C',['israel','teach','sign'],["Matthew 4:23–25","Luke 4:42–44"],
 "He goes about all Galilee “teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom”, and healing. He tells those who would keep Him, “I must preach the kingdom of God to other cities also: for therefore am I sent.”",["The text names no towns here; the marker is the map’s placement in central Galilee."]),
('mountain1','C','The mountain: the Twelve and the sermon',32.880,35.550,'I',['israel','teach'],["Luke 6:12–19","Matthew 5:1–12","Matthew 7:28–29"],
 "He spends the night in prayer, chooses twelve and names them apostles, then teaches the multitude. Matthew places the sermon on “a mountain”, and Luke on “the plain”",["Neither Gospel names the mountain; the marker is the map’s placement near Capernaum.","Whether the Sermon on the Mount and the sermon on the plain are one discourse or two is not stated."]),
('capernaum2','C','Capernaum: the centurion and Jairus',32.881,35.575,'S',['gentile','sign'],["Matthew 8:5–13","Mark 5:21–43"],
 "A centurion asks for his servant, and the Lord says, “I have not found so great faith, no, not in Israel.” Later a ruler of the synagogue, Jairus, asks for his daughter, and she is raised.",["The Gospels do not fix where Jairus’s house stood; the map places it at Capernaum by Matthew 9:1 and Mark 5:21."]),
('nain','C','Nain',32.633,35.349,'S',['israel','sign'],["Luke 7:11–17"],
 "He raises a widow’s only son at the gate of the city, and the people say, “God hath visited his people.”",[]),
('gerasenes','C','The east shore: the Gadarenes',32.829,35.651,'I',['gentile','sign'],["Mark 4:35–41","Mark 5:1–20"],
 "He calms the storm, then crosses to the country of the Gadarenes, where He sends the legion out of a man into the swine. The man is told to tell his friends what the Lord has done, and he publishes it in Decapolis.",["Matthew calls the country Gergesenes, Mark Gadarenes (Gerasenes in some texts) and Luke Gadarenes. The marker is a traditional site on the east shore."]),
('nazareth2','C','Nazareth: His own country',32.699,35.304,'S',['israel','teach'],["Mark 6:1–6"],
 "He teaches in the synagogue on the sabbath; they are astonished and take offence. “A prophet is not without honour, but in his own country.” He “marvelled because of their unbelief.”",["See the note at the Nazareth synagogue: the map reads this as a second visit."]),
('twelve','C','Galilee: the Twelve sent',32.780,35.420,'C',['israel','teach','sign'],["Matthew 10:1–15","Mark 6:7–13"],
 "He sends the Twelve in pairs with power over unclean spirits. “Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not: But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.”",["The villages are not named; the marker is the map’s placement in Galilee."]),
('bethsaida1','C','Bethsaida: the five thousand',32.909,35.631,'S',['israel','sign','feast'],["Luke 9:10–17","John 6:1–15"],
 "The Twelve return, and He takes them aside to “a desert place belonging to the city called Bethsaida.” John writes that the passover “was nigh.” He feeds the multitude, and they would take Him by force to make Him a king; He departs.",[]),
('gennesaret','C','The sea and Gennesaret',32.850,35.520,'S',['sign'],["Matthew 14:22–36"],
 "He sends the disciples across, prays on the mountain, and comes to them on the sea. Peter walks to Him and begins to sink. In the land of Gennesaret all that touch the hem of His garment are made perfectly whole.",[]),
('capernaum3','C','Capernaum: the bread of life',32.881,35.575,'S',['israel','teach'],["John 6:22–71","John 7:1"],
 "In the synagogue He teaches, “I am the bread of life.” Many of His disciples go back and walk no more with Him, and Peter answers, “Thou hast the words of eternal life.” John then writes that He “walked in Galilee” and would not walk in Jewry (7:1).",[]),
('tyre','D','Tyre and Sidon',33.273,35.196,'S',['gentile','sign'],["Matthew 15:21–28"],
 "He departs into the coasts of Tyre and Sidon. A woman of Canaan cries after Him; He says, “I am not sent but unto the lost sheep of the house of Israel,” and then, at her answer, “O woman, great is thy faith: be it unto thee even as thou wilt.”",["Mark 7:24–30 calls her a Greek, a Syrophenician by nation. The map keeps both descriptions."]),
('decapolis','D','Decapolis',32.780,35.650,'S',['gentile','sign'],["Mark 7:31–37","Matthew 15:29–39"],
 "He returns “through the midst of the coasts of Decapolis,” heals a deaf man with an impediment, and feeds four thousand. The multitude “glorified the God of Israel.”",[]),
('magdala','D','Magdala (Dalmanutha)',32.826,35.517,'I',['israel'],["Matthew 15:39–16:4","Mark 8:10–13"],
 "He comes into the coasts of Magdala, and the Pharisees and Sadducees ask for a sign. He answers that there will be given them “the sign of the prophet Jonas.”",["Matthew names Magdala and Mark names Dalmanutha; the site of Dalmanutha is not known."]),
('bethsaida2','D','Bethsaida: the blind man',32.909,35.631,'S',['sign'],["Mark 8:22–26"],
 "He leads a blind man out of the town and heals him in two stages: “I see men as trees, walking.”",[]),
('caesarea','D','Caesarea Philippi',33.248,35.694,'S',['israel','teach'],["Matthew 16:13–28"],
 "He asks, “Whom do men say that I the Son of man am?” and Peter answers, “Thou art the Christ, the Son of the living God.” From that time He begins to show His disciples that He must go to Jerusalem, suffer and be killed, and be raised the third day.",["The Gospels do not say whether the confession is also a turn toward the Cross for the multitudes; the map reports the text."]),
('transfig','D','The high mountain',33.320,35.760,'I',['israel','sign'],["Matthew 17:1–9"],
 "Six days after, He takes Peter, James and John up “an high mountain apart” and is transfigured: “his face did shine as the sun.” Moses and Elias talk with Him, and a voice from the cloud says, “hear ye him.”",["No Gospel names the mountain. The marker is the map’s placement in the region of Caesarea Philippi, not a claim about the site."]),
('galilee2','D','Galilee and Capernaum',32.881,35.575,'S',['israel','teach'],["Matthew 17:22–27"],
 "While they abide in Galilee He tells them again that He will be delivered and killed and rise the third day. At Capernaum He sends Peter to catch a fish with a coin in its mouth for the tribute.",[]),
('samaria','E','A village of the Samaritans',32.450,35.300,'I',['gentile'],["Luke 9:51–56"],
 "“When the time was come that he should be received up, he stedfastly set his face to go to Jerusalem.” A village of the Samaritans will not receive Him; the disciples ask for fire, and He rebukes them.",["The village is not named; the marker is the map’s placement on the road through Samaria."]),
('tabernacles','E','Jerusalem: the feast of tabernacles',31.778,35.235,'S',['israel','feast','teach','sign'],["John 7:2–14","John 7:37–39","John 8:12","John 9:1–7"],
 "He goes up to the feast of tabernacles, “not openly, but as it were in secret”, and teaches in the temple. “In the last day, that great day of the feast,” He cries, “If any man thirst, let him come unto me, and drink.” He says, “I am the light of the world,” and gives sight to a man blind from his birth.",["The map places these John 7–10 events in Jerusalem within the journey Luke 9:51 begins; the Gospels do not state how the two accounts interlock."]),
('bethany1','E','Bethany: Martha and Mary',31.771,35.262,'I',['teach'],["Luke 10:38–42","John 11:1–3"],
 "He is received into the house of Martha, and Mary sits at His feet. “One thing is needful: and Mary hath chosen that good part.”",["Luke does not name the village; John 11:1 names Bethany as the town of Mary and Martha. The map joins them (Inferred)."]),
('dedication','E','Jerusalem: the feast of dedication',31.778,35.235,'S',['israel','feast','teach'],["John 10:22–39"],
 "It is “the feast of the dedication… and it was winter.” In Solomon’s porch the Jews ask Him to say plainly whether He is the Christ. He answers, “I and my Father are one,” and they take up stones.",[]),
('beyondjordan1','E','Beyond Jordan',31.837,35.551,'I',['israel'],["John 10:40–42"],
 "He goes away again beyond Jordan into “the place where John at first baptized”, and many resort to Him and believe.",[]),
('bethany2','E','Bethany: Lazarus',31.771,35.262,'S',['sign'],["John 11:1–46"],
 "Lazarus is four days in the grave when He comes. He says, “I am the resurrection, and the life,” weeps, and calls Lazarus out. The chief priests and Pharisees then gather a council (11:47–53).",[]),
('ephraim','E','Ephraim',31.950,35.280,'I',[],["John 11:54"],
 "He walks no more openly among the Jews, but goes “a country near to the wilderness, into a city called Ephraim,” and continues there with His disciples.",["The site of Ephraim is not settled; the marker is the traditional one."]),
('border','F','Between Samaria and Galilee',32.550,35.450,'I',['gentile','sign'],["Luke 17:11–19"],
 "As He goes to Jerusalem He passes “through the midst of Samaria and Galilee” and cleanses ten lepers; the one who returns to give thanks is a Samaritan.",[]),
('perea','F','Beyond Jordan: toward Judaea',31.950,35.650,'I',['israel','teach'],["Matthew 19:1–2","Mark 10:1–2","Mark 10:32–34"],
 "He comes into the coasts of Judaea “beyond Jordan”, and the multitudes follow. On the way up He tells the Twelve a third time that the Son of man will be delivered, condemned and killed, and rise the third day.",["The map’s order of this stop and the border stop is its own reading."]),
('jericho','F','Jericho',31.870,35.443,'S',['israel','sign','teach'],["Luke 18:35–43","Luke 19:1–10","Luke 19:11–27"],
 "Near Jericho a blind man receives sight, and in the city Zacchaeus receives Him: “This day is salvation come to this house, forsomuch as he also is a son of Abraham.” Because “they thought that the kingdom of God should immediately appear,” He tells the parable of the pounds.",["Mark 10:46 and Matthew 20:29 place the blind men as He leaves Jericho and Luke as He draws near. The map reports the difference and does not settle it."]),
('bethany3','F','Bethany: six days before the Passover',31.771,35.262,'S',['sign'],["John 12:1–11"],
 "Six days before the passover He comes to Bethany; Mary anoints His feet with ointment and wipes them with her hair. Many of the Jews come to see Lazarus, and the chief priests consult to put Lazarus also to death.",["Matthew 26:6–13 and Mark 14:3–9 place an anointing in the house of Simon the leper, two days before the Passover. The map does not say whether these are one anointing or two."]),
('olivet','F','Bethphage and the Mount of Olives',31.775,35.255,'S',['israel','feast'],["Matthew 21:1–11","Luke 19:37–44"],
 "He sends two disciples for the colt and rides toward Jerusalem; the multitude cries, “Hosanna to the son of David.” Matthew quotes Zechariah 9:9, “Behold, thy King cometh unto thee.” As He draws near and sees the city, He weeps over it.",[]),
('jerusalem7','G','Jerusalem: the last week',31.778,35.235,'S',['israel','feast','teach','sign'],["Matthew 21:12–17"],
 "He enters the temple, drives out the sellers and heals the blind and the lame, then lodges at Bethany. The week that follows is set out day by day in the panel.",["The Gospels do not give every day; Wednesday is not described, and the map does not fill it."]),
('emmaus','H','The road to Emmaus',31.800,35.100,'I',[],["Luke 24:13–35"],
 "On the first day two disciples go toward Emmaus, “about threescore furlongs” from Jerusalem, and the risen Lord joins them. “Beginning at Moses and all the prophets, he expounded unto them in all the scriptures the things concerning himself.” He is known in the breaking of bread.",["The site of Emmaus is not known. The marker is the map’s placement west of Jerusalem."]),
('galilee3','H','Galilee, after the resurrection',32.850,35.580,'C',['israel'],["John 21:1–14","Matthew 28:16–20"],
 "At the sea of Tiberias He shows Himself to seven disciples and eats with them. Matthew records the eleven going into Galilee, “into a mountain where Jesus had appointed them”, and the commission He gives them.",["The mountain is not named. The commission is reported as written, to the eleven in Galilee; the map does not apply it beyond the audience the text names."]),
('olivet2','H','Bethany and the Mount of Olives',31.771,35.262,'S',[],["Luke 24:50–53","Acts 1:3–12"],
 "After forty days He leads them out as far as Bethany, lifts up His hands, blesses them and is carried up into heaven. Two men in white say, “this same Jesus, which is taken up from you into heaven, shall so come in like manner as ye have seen him go into heaven.”",["The Second Coming Map takes up that coming."]),
]
# The last week in Jerusalem (cluster panel): (day, title, refs, happens)
WEEK=[
 ("First day of the week","Entry and the temple",["Matthew 21:1–11","Mark 11:11"],"He enters the city, goes into the temple, looks round about, and goes out to Bethany with the Twelve (Mark 11:11)."),
 ("Next day","The fig tree and the temple",["Mark 11:12–19"],"On the way from Bethany He curses a fig tree; in the temple He overturns the tables of the moneychangers and says, “My house shall be called of all nations the house of prayer.”"),
 ("Next day","Teaching in the temple",["Mark 11:20–12:44"],"The fig tree is found withered; the chief priests, scribes and elders ask by what authority He acts; He answers with parables and with questions from the Pharisees, Sadducees and a scribe, and observes the widow’s two mites."),
 ("The same day","The Mount of Olives",["Matthew 24:1–3","Mark 13:1–4"],"As He leaves the temple the disciples show Him its buildings; on the mount of Olives they ask when these things shall be, and what the sign of His coming is. The Second Coming Map takes up the coming He describes."),
 ("Two days before the Passover","The plot",["Matthew 26:1–5","Matthew 26:14–16"],"The chief priests and elders gather at the palace of Caiaphas; Judas goes to them and covenants for thirty pieces of silver."),
 ("The first day of unleavened bread","The upper room",["Luke 22:7–38","John 13:1–17"],"He sends Peter and John to prepare the passover, sits down with the twelve apostles, gives the bread and the cup, and washes their feet. John 14–17 records what He says after."),
 ("That night","Gethsemane",["Matthew 26:36–56"],"He prays in Gethsemane, “not as I will, but as thou wilt.” Judas comes with a great multitude, and the disciples forsake Him and flee."),
 ("That night","The high priest and Peter",["Matthew 26:57–75","John 18:12–27"],"He is led to Annas and to Caiaphas; false witnesses are sought; He is condemned as guilty of death. Peter denies Him three times."),
 ("The morning","Pilate and Herod",["John 18:28–19:16","Luke 23:6–12"],"He is taken to the judgment hall of Pilate, who finds no fault in Him, sends Him to Herod, and at last delivers Him to be crucified."),
 ("The third hour to the ninth","Golgotha",["John 19:17–30","Luke 23:33–46"],"He is crucified with two others at a place called Golgotha. He says, “It is finished,” and gives up the ghost."),
 ("The same evening","The tomb",["John 19:38–42","Matthew 27:57–66"],"Joseph of Arimathaea and Nicodemus lay Him in a new tomb in the garden; the chief priests ask Pilate for a watch."),
 ("The first day of the week","The empty tomb",["Matthew 28:1–10","John 20:1–18"],"Mary Magdalene and the other Mary come at the dawn; the stone is rolled away, and the Lord meets them. That evening He stands in the midst of the disciples (John 20:19–23)."),
]
WEEK_SILENT=["Whether the passover He eats in the upper room is the same meal the Jews eat the next day is not stated: the Synoptic Gospels (Mark 14:12) and John (18:28; 19:14) are read side by side, and the map does not reconcile them.",
 "The Gospels do not describe Wednesday, and the map does not fill it."]
GLOBAL_SILENT=[
 "A date for the ministry. John names the Passover at 2:13, 6:4 and 11:55, and an unnamed feast at 5:1; the map reads these as at least a little over two years and does not assign years.",
 "A single order of the Gospel events. Where the Gospels differ, the map follows Matthew and Mark for Galilee and John for the feasts, and says so where it matters.",
 "The sites of Bethabara, Aenon, Dalmanutha, Ephraim, the mountains of the sermon and the transfiguration, and Emmaus.",
 "That the Lord’s ministry before the Cross was to Israel only. The text records Gentile and Samaritan contacts, and the map marks them and reports both Matthew 10:5–6 and John 4.",
]
